 ## Overview
-DropGaussian is a structural regularization technique for sparse-view 3D Gaussian Splatting that randomly removes a subset of Gaussians during the training process to alleviate overfitting to the limited number of training views [S1]. It sets itself apart from prior-based methods (which use external depth signals or 2D generative models) by being prior-free, requiring no additional computational costs, external data, or complex modules [S1]. Unlike standard 3DGS, which suffers from overfitting in sparse-view settings due to low gradient feedback for occluded or distant Gaussians, DropGaussian improves the visibility and gradient magnitude of remaining Gaussians by temporarily excluding closer ones [S1].
+DropGaussian is a structural regularization technique designed to mitigate overfitting in 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) when trained on a sparse set of input views [S1]. It operates by randomly excluding a subset of Gaussians from the rendering and gradient computation during training, while simultaneously scaling the opacity of the remaining Gaussians to preserve the total color contribution [S1]. Unlike prior-based methods that rely on external depth signals or 2D generative models, DropGaussian is prior-free and introduces no additional computational complexity or external data requirements [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-- **Inputs:** The set of 3D Gaussians $\mathcal{G}$ (position, covariance, opacity, spherical harmonics coefficients) from the base 3D Gaussian Splatting framework [S1]; a target training view image; a current iteration index $t$; and a total iteration count $t_{\text{total}}$ [S1].
-- **Outputs:** Gradients for the non-dropped Gaussians; modified opacity values for the non-dropped Gaussians used in the forward pass [S1].
+DropGaussian modifies the standard 3DGS training loop by introducing a stochastic dropout mechanism applied to the Gaussian primitives. The method addresses the issue where Gaussians far from the camera or occluded in sparse views receive insufficient gradient feedback, leading to overfitting [S1]. By randomly removing closer Gaussians, the visibility of distant Gaussians is improved, allowing them to receive larger gradients and contribute more effectively to the optimization [S1].
 
